# +Count, +Edges, +Budget, -Term
# +Count, +Edges, +Budget, -Lengths
# +Count, +Edges, -Length
# ?Point, ?Count, ?Extra, ?Seed, ?Slack
#   * <module> The shortest walk that visits every node
#
# THE PROBLEM (LeetCode 847, "Shortest Path Visiting All Nodes"): an
# undirected graph; the least number of edges of a walk that visits every
# node, starting anywhere, nodes and edges may be revisited. Asked as the set
# of the lengths at most T of the walks that stop when they have first
# visited every node; T is the least, so the set is that one number.
#
# The program writes top (corpora/library/top) where the pseudo-code and the
# argument below write {}, the empty choice of the calculus that still has
# it; doc/proofs/reduction_series_answers.md carries the argument over to
# Definition 14, and the generator is the family's Scala object in
# gezi/contest-corpora.
#
# THE PROGRAM, in continuation passing style, the visited nodes a bit set
# (the prelude's bit_set_full, bit_set_insert; the API of
# round-37-directions.md):
#
#     walk v c r k = full c (k r)
#                      { r {} (\p. walk u (insert c u) p k) | u a neighbour of v }
#     program      = { walk s (insert none s) T (\r. difference T r) | s a node }
#
# The neighbours of v are a nested case on the unary numeral v (the
# program's own code), each branch the set of v's neighbours' steps.
# Directions: A (the visited nodes as a bit set; a table keyed by the node,
# the bit set and the budget is the search over (subset, node) pairs); C (the
# graph has cycles, walks go round them, the budget bounds them); F (a trap
# whose graph is disconnected has an empty snapshot for every budget).
#
# THE SNAPSHOT. S(P) is the set of the lengths at most T of the walks that
# visit every node and stop at the first step at which they have:
# visit_all_lengths/4 on small graphs. Every such walk has at least the least
# length (shortest_visit_all/3, a breadth-first search over (subset, node)
# pairs), so for T that least length S(P) is {T}, and for T one less it is
# empty.
#
# THE LEMMAS, on the definitions of the library functions (contest_attractors
# attractor_source/2 and the prelude), not on their native references.
# ⌜n⌝ is the Scott unary numeral, ⌜0⌝ = λz.λs. z and ⌜n + 1⌝ = λz.λs. s ⌜n⌝;
# true = λt.λf. t and false = λt.λf. f; a bit set is a Scott list of
# Booleans, cons h t one β step from λn.λc. c h t, and none is the list of
# N falses (none_collected/2), N the number of nodes. The lets of
# numeral_lets/4 bind node 0 to ⌜0⌝ and node i + 1 to λz.λs. s (node i), so
# after their β steps node i is ⌜i⌝ and the budgets are ⌜T⌝ (unary/2).
# - (Y) fixpoint G =β G (fixpoint G), two β steps.
# - (Case) numeral_case [C0, ..., Cm] v is v C0 (λp. numeral_case
#   [C1, ..., Cm] p), so for v =β ⌜i⌝, i =< m, it is β-equal to Ci, by
#   induction on i: ⌜0⌝ selects C0 and ⌜i + 1⌝ gives the case of ⌜i⌝ among
#   the rest.
# - (Insert) insert = Y (λs.λc.λi. c nil (λb.λrest. i (cons true rest)
#   (λp. cons b (s rest p)))). For a bit set c of N elements and i < N,
#   insert c ⌜i⌝ is β-equal to c with its element i made true, by induction
#   on i: ⌜0⌝ gives cons true rest, and ⌜i + 1⌝ keeps the head and inserts i
#   into the rest.
# - (Full) full = Y (λs.λc. c true (λb.λrest. b (s rest) false)). For a bit
#   set c of Booleans, full c =β true when every element is true and false
#   otherwise, by induction on the list: nil gives true; cons b rest gives
#   full rest when b is true and false when b is false.
# - (Budget) r {} (λp. M) with r =β ⌜j⌝ is β-equal to {} when j = 0 and to
#   M[p := ⌜j - 1⌝] when j > 0.
# - (Difference) difference = Y (λs.λm.λn. n m (λn1. m ⌜0⌝ (λm1. s m1 n1))).
#   By induction on n, difference ⌜m⌝ ⌜n⌝ =β ⌜m - n⌝ when m ≥ n: n = 0
#   gives ⌜m⌝, and n = k + 1, m = j + 1 give difference ⌜j⌝ ⌜k⌝.
# - (Empty) If U →* {} by β and η steps, no pure term is reached from U by
#   →c steps. Every →c step keeps U →* {}: a β or η step by confluence, {}
#   being normal; a member replacement of a set s by Lemma 2a of
#   certificate_meta_lemmas.md, which replays U →* {} to {} with the
#   descendants of s replaced, and {} is no descendant of s, s having a
#   member and descendants keeping the number of members. A pure term
#   reduces only to pure terms, and {} is not pure.
#
# By (Insert), along a walk v0, ..., vi the bit set is β-equal to the list
# whose element u is true exactly when u is among v0..vi, and by (Full) full
# of it is true exactly when the walk has visited every node.
#
# PROOF, with ⟦·⟧, D, S and Th and Lemma 2b of
# gezi/doc/proofs/certificate_meta_lemmas.md.
# - (⊇) For such a walk s = v0, v1, ..., vm with m =< T: choose the member of
#   s at the head; unfold walk by (Y); at each vi, i < m, the walk has not
#   yet visited every node, so full selects the neighbour set (Full), which
#   is the case of vi (Case); choose the member of vi+1 and take the
#   budget's successor case (Budget), the budget ⌜T - i⌝ with T - i > 0. At
#   vm full selects k r (Full), r =β ⌜T - m⌝, and k r is
#   difference ⌜T⌝ ⌜T - m⌝ =β ⌜m⌝ (Difference), pure and normal: ⌜m⌝ is in
#   that term's meaning (equal terms, Lemma 2b.2) and so in ⟦P⟧ (Lemma
#   2b.2).
# - (⊆) By Lemma 2b.3 every yield equals a pure term P reaches by a finite
#   sequence σ. P's sets are the outer set, the neighbour sets and {}; σ
#   removes them only by members and {} never, and each chosen branch is the
#   whole term, every call of walk being in tail position. Where the budget
#   is ⌜0⌝ its case is β-equal to {} (Budget), and so is the case of a node
#   with no neighbour (Case, an empty set of steps), and no pure term is
#   reached from either (Empty). So a pure term is reached only along a walk
#   from a start, each step to a neighbour (Case), paid by the budget's
#   successor case (Budget), until the walk has first visited every node
#   (Full, which selects k r only then); the term is then β-equal to
#   difference ⌜T⌝ ⌜T - m⌝, m the walk's length, so to ⌜m⌝ (Difference),
#   and m =< T.
#
# THE SUBTASKS AND THE TRAPS: visit_point/5 below; the graph is a random spanning
# tree plus Extra random edges. The traps: T one less than the
# least (empty), and a graph with an isolated node (empty for every T; a
# setting that answers some length there is wrong).
#
# TARGET ALGORITHM: breadth-first search over the pairs of a subset and a
# node, the subset a bitmask (M. Held, R. M. Karp, J. SIAM 1962,
# doi:10.1137/0110015, for the subset states; R. Bellman, Quarterly of
# Applied Mathematics 1958, doi:10.1090/qam/102435, whose recursion on unit
# weights is the layered search).

#   THE SUBTASKS: visit_point(?Point, ?Count, ?Extra, ?Seed, ?Slack), Count
#   nodes on a random spanning tree plus Extra random edges, the budget the least
#   length plus the Slack's amount; the Slack is a constructor of
#   hidden_37_visit_all/slack, added with its amount, or isolated, the last
#   node losing its edges.
#   Sized by a probe of 2026-09-30 (CPUs 0-7, 1024 MB stack and
#   table, the machine shared at load average 12), each reading equal to
#   its snapshot: tabling(weak_head) reads five (the least length 6) in
#   1169 ms and seven (8) in 38380 ms, and is stopped at 60 s on nine (11)
#   and twelve (14); the traps read nothing, in 15027 ms (one short) and
#   25261 ms (isolated). The native reference shortest_visit_all/3 answers
#   every point in 27 ms or less.
#
